###  https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDuration
<https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDuration> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                    skos:Concept ;
                                                           obo:IAO_0000119 "The duration axis is the original scale-reading distinction of Gilbert, C.H. 1913, Age at maturity of the Pacific coast salmon of the genus Oncorhynchus, Bulletin of the United States Bureau of Fisheries 32:1-22. Its modern statement for Sockeye Salmon, in freshwater-annulus terms, is Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713."@en ;
                                                           dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713> ;
                                                           rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/smn> ;
                                                           skos:definition "The length of time juveniles of a salmon group remain in fresh water between emergence and seaward migration, expressed as a controlled value."@en ;
                                                           skos:inScheme <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDurationScheme> ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "Juvenile freshwater residence duration"@en ;
                                                           skos:topConceptOf <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDurationScheme> .


###  https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDurationScheme
<https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDurationScheme> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                          skos:ConceptScheme ;
                                                                 obo:IAO_0000119 "The duration axis is the original scale-reading distinction of Gilbert, C.H. 1913, Age at maturity of the Pacific coast salmon of the genus Oncorhynchus, Bulletin of the United States Bureau of Fisheries 32:1-22, whose sea type and stream type separate fish by whether the scale nucleus records a freshwater winter. Its modern statement for Sockeye Salmon, in freshwater-annulus terms, is Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, Population structure of sea-type and lake-type sockeye salmon and kokanee in the Fraser River and Columbia River drainages, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713."@en ;
                                                                 dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713> ;
                                                                 rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/smn> ;
                                                                 skos:definition "Controlled vocabulary for the duration axis of juvenile salmon life history: how long juveniles remain in fresh water between emergence and seaward migration."@en ;
                                                                 skos:hasTopConcept <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileFreshwaterResidenceDuration> ;
                                                                 skos:prefLabel "Juvenile freshwater residence duration scheme"@en ;
                                                                 skos:scopeNote "Species-neutral by construction. This is an axis a named life-history type is defined from, not a life-history type itself: a value here carries no claim about where juveniles reared, how adults behave, or what genetic structure the group has. It is also not age at maturity, which Healey (1991) treats as tactical variation within a race rather than as race-defining. The scheme is deliberately coarse — a two-valued cut at the first freshwater winter — because that cut is what a scale reader can actually determine: a fish that overwinters in fresh water lays down a freshwater annulus and one that does not, does not."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitat
<https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitat> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                       skos:Concept ;
                                              obo:IAO_0000119 "The lake-versus-riverine rearing distinction in Sockeye Salmon is stated by Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713."@en ;
                                              dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713> ;
                                              rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/smn> ;
                                              skos:definition "The kind of rearing environment in which juveniles of a salmon group grow before migrating to sea, expressed as a controlled value."@en ;
                                              skos:inScheme <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitatScheme> ;
                                              skos:prefLabel "Juvenile rearing habitat"@en ;
                                              skos:topConceptOf <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitatScheme> .


###  https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitatScheme
<https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitatScheme> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                             skos:ConceptScheme ;
                                                    obo:IAO_0000119 "The lake-versus-riverine rearing distinction in Sockeye Salmon is drawn by Wood, C.C., Bickham, J.W., Nelson, R.J., Foote, C.J. and Patton, J.C. 2008, Recurrent evolution of life history ecotypes in sockeye salmon, Evolutionary Applications 1:207-221, who group the river-type and sea-type forms together because, by definition, neither rears in lakes; Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713, page 2, report that grouping. It is also the cut DFO itself makes: the glossary of Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, DFO Can. Sci. Advis. Sec. Res. Doc. 2007/070, defines the river/ocean-types of sockeye as juveniles adapted to rearing in flowing water instead of lakes."@en ;
                                                    dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713> ;
                                                    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/smn> ;
                                                    skos:definition "Controlled vocabulary for the habitat axis of juvenile salmon life history: the kind of rearing environment juveniles occupy between emergence and seaward migration."@en ;
                                                    skos:hasTopConcept <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitat> ;
                                                    skos:prefLabel "Juvenile rearing habitat scheme"@en ;
                                                    skos:scopeNote "Species-neutral by construction, and independent of the duration axis: the same kind of rearing habitat is used by groups that leave in their first year and by groups that stay a year or more, which is why the two axes cross-classify rather than nest. Values are kinds of water, never named water bodies; a property relating a population to an actual lake or river is a different property and is not minted here. Do not populate this scheme with the potamodromous vocabulary of Rieman and McIntyre (1993), who divide migratory bull trout into fluvial and adfluvial (or lacustrine) forms beside the resident form, despite lacustrine resembling lake and fluvial resembling river. That vocabulary classifies migration completed within fresh water and has no anadromous category, because anadromy had not been demonstrated in bull trout, so calling an anadromous Sockeye population adfluvial is a category error. Applying it to non-anadromous Oncorhynchus nerka (kokanee) is defensible and would be a different scheme."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/smn/LakeRearingHabitat
<https://w3id.org/smn/LakeRearingHabitat> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                   skos:Concept ;
                                          obo:IAO_0000119 "Beacham, T.D. and Withler, R.E. 2017, PLOS ONE 12(9):e0183713, page 1, whose lake-type Sockeye Salmon rear in nursery lakes. Stalberg, H.C., Lauzier, R.B., MacIsaac, E.A., Porter, M. and Murray, C. 2009, Canada's Policy for Conservation of Wild Pacific Salmon: Stream, Lake, and Estuarine Habitat Indicators, Can. Manuscr. Rep. Fish. Aquat. Sci. 2859, section 3.1.2, page 17, for lakes as rearing habitat of Chinook, coho and sockeye salmon."@en ;
                                          dcterms:source <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0183713> ;
                                          rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/smn> ;
                                          skos:broader <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitat> ;
                                          skos:definition "Rearing habitat in which juveniles grow in a lake, such as the nursery lake of lake-type Sockeye Salmon or a lake used for rearing by juvenile Chinook or coho salmon."@en ;
                                          skos:inScheme <https://w3id.org/smn/JuvenileRearingHabitatScheme> ;
                                          skos:prefLabel "Lake rearing habitat"@en .


